    "exampleHtml": "My boss <span class=\"vp-verb\">makes</span> us <span class=\"vp-prep\">work</span> late. · My parents <span class=\"vp-verb\">let</span> me <span class=\"vp-prep\">stay</span> out. · She <span class=\"vp-verb\">helped</span> me <span class=\"vp-prep\">carry</span> the bags.",
    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "<strong>make + someone + base verb</strong> means the person has no choice, or something causes a feeling or a reaction. <em>My boss makes us work late. The film made me cry. Don't make me laugh!</em>",
        "tag": "make"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>let + someone + base verb</strong> means to allow it. <em>My parents let me stay out late. Our teacher doesn't let us use phones. Let me help you.</em>",
        "tag": "let"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>help + someone + base verb</strong> means to give a hand. <em>to</em> is optional: <em>She helped me carry the bags. She helped me to carry the bags.</em> Both are fine. The short form is more common.",
        "tag": "help"
      },
      {
        "html": "Typical mistakes: ✗ <em>My boss makes us to work late.</em> → ✓ <em>My boss makes us work late.</em> · ✗ <em>My parents let me to stay out.</em> → ✓ <em>My parents let me stay out.</em> · ✗ <em>My mum made me tidying my room.</em> → ✓ <em>My mum made me tidy my room.</em>",
        "tag": "Typical mistakes"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "<strong>Be careful with the passive.</strong> <em>make</em> keeps its <em>to</em> in the passive: <em>They made us wait. → We were made to wait.</em> <em>let</em> has no passive in everyday English. Use <em>be allowed to</em> instead: <em>My parents let me go out. → I was allowed to go out.</em> Remember that <em>Let's</em> is short for <em>let us</em>: <em>Let's go!</em> Other verbs with someone need <em>to</em>: <em>want, tell, ask</em> (<em>She told me to wait</em>). See <a href=\"/verb-ing-or-to\">-ing or to</a>. The verbs <em>see</em> and <em>hear</em> also work without <em>to</em>: <em>I heard him sing.</em> For <em>have something done</em>, see <a href=\"/have-something-done\">Have / get something done</a>. After <em>can, must</em> and <em>should</em>, the base verb is also used: <a href=\"/modal-verbs\">Modal verbs</a>.",
    "reference": {
      "showLabel": "Show overview ▼",
      "hideLabel": "Hide overview ▲",
      "tables": [
